lisa-design:
Leading "/" and "./" in tar entry names and hardlink targets are archive-root relative (GNU tar behaviour; `opa build` writes every entry as "/name"). They are stripped BEFORE path.Clean so "/../x" becomes "../x" and is still rejected; cleaning first would turn it into "/x" and silently accept it.
Symlink targets are deliberately NOT normalised: an absolute symlink target is resolved from the real filesystem root when followed, so it stays rejected.
A hardlink to "/etc/passwd" is now accepted as archive-root "etc/passwd"; links are never created, so this only removes a false rejection.
Volume names ("C:\x") stay rejected via isAbsolutePath on the normalised name. Error messages for still-rejected cases are unchanged and quote the original entry name.
